#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221109 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32e63c33-8820-542f-afab-f27a1f6a847a" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Smoothness Property"}] :question/id #uuid "f0be4adc-84af-538c-a6b6-ae0716e66cb3" :question/math-academy-id "e-21646" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for $s > a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s > a,$ and we have\n\n$$\nF''(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{ t^2\\cdot f(t) \\}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\:$ $F(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t$ exists for $s > a.$\n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2}{\\textrm{d}s^2}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t$\n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty \\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}\\big(e^{-st} f(t)\\big)\\,\\textrm{d}t$\n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\:$ $\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}\\big(e^{-st} f(t)\\big) = t^2 e^{-st} f(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L5}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty t^2 e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t$\n\n$\\textrm{L6}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{t^2\\cdot f(t)\\}$\n\nExplain the reasoning behind:\n\n- How line $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}.$\n\n- How line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from its preceding line.\n\n- How line $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from $\\textrm{L5}.$" :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s > a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\geq 0$ and $s > a,$ we have\n\n$$\nF^{(n)}(s) = (-1)^n\\, \\mathcal{L}\\{ t^n f(t) \\}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n = 2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\textrm{L2}.$ From line $\\textrm{L1}$ we know that\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nBy the definition of the second derivative with respect to $s,$ we get\n\n$$\nF''(s) = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2}{\\mathrm{d}s^2} F(s) = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2}{\\mathrm{d}s^2}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the statement in line $\\textrm{L2}.$\n\n- We now consider line $\\textrm{L4}.$ The function $f(t)$ does not depend on $s$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}\\big(e^{-st} f(t)\\big)\n  = f(t)\\,\\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}(e^{-st})\n  = f(t)\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial s}(-te^{-st})\n  = f(t)\\,(t^2 e^{-st})\n  = t^2 e^{-st} f(t).\n$$\n\nThus line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from the chain rule.\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\textrm{L6}.$ By the definition of the Laplace transform,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{t^2\\cdot f(t)\\}\n  = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} \\big(t^2 f(t)\\big)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the integral in line $\\textrm{L5}$, we see that\n\n$$\nF''(s)\n  = \\int_0^\\infty t^2 e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n  = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} \\big(t^2 f(t)\\big)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n  = \\mathcal{L}\\{t^2\\cdot f(t)\\},\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the expression in line $\\textrm{L6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221136 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7114bb44-4dd5-5b2b-86dc-f81761c0e07a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms Using the Smoothness Property (1st Derivative)"}] :question/id #uuid "fac2d0c3-b5e2-5683-ab0a-74fc8d8bf136" :question/math-academy-id "e-21834" :question/problem "Use the smoothness property to find the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{t \\cos 5t\\}.\n$$\n\n \nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L \\{\\cos \\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad s > 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s > a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\geq 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{ t^n f(t) \\} = (-1)^n F^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{ tf(t) \\} = -F'(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t) = \\cos 5t.$ Using the given result with $\\omega = 5,$ the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+25},\n\\qquad s > 0.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(s({s}^{2}+25)^{-1}) \\\\  & =({s}^{2}+25)^{-1}+s(-({s}^{2}+25)^{-2}\\cdot 2s) \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+25}{({s}^{2}+25)^{2}}-\\frac{2{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+25)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+25)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t}=-{F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}-25}{({s}^{2}+25)^{2}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221140 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6373d849-f5c6-50d2-ad4f-72f77a1a8c92" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms Using the Smoothness Property (2nd Derivative)"}] :question/id #uuid "07ff9076-2942-574e-98ae-fdd80d69109f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21838" :question/problem "Use the smoothness property to find the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{t^2 \\sinh{5t}\\}.\n$$\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L \\{\\sinh \\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2-\\omega^2}, \\qquad s > \\omega.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s > a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\geq 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{ t^n f(t) \\} = (-1)^n F^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{ t^2 f(t) \\} = F''(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t) = \\sinh 5t.$ Using the given result with $\\omega = 5,$ the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{5}{s^2-25},\n\\qquad s > 5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}-25}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(5({s}^{2}-25)^{-1}) \\\\  & =-5({s}^{2}-25)^{-2}\\cdot 2s \\\\  & =-\\frac{10s}{({s}^{2}-25)^{2}} \\\\ {F}^{″}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{10s}{({s}^{2}-25)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-10\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}-25)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-10\\cdot (\\frac{(s)^{′}\\cdot ({s}^{2}-25)^{2}-s\\cdot {(({s}^{2}-25)^{2})}^{′}}{({s}^{2}-25)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-10\\cdot (\\frac{({s}^{2}-25)^{2}-s\\cdot 2({s}^{2}-25)\\cdot 2s}{({s}^{2}-25)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-10\\cdot (\\frac{({s}^{2}-25)^{2}-4{s}^{2}({s}^{2}-25)}{({s}^{2}-25)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-10\\cdot (\\frac{{s}^{2}-25-4{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}-25)^{3}}) \\\\  & =-10\\cdot (\\frac{-3{s}^{2}-25}{({s}^{2}-25)^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30{s}^{2}+250}{({s}^{2}-25)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the quotient rule to find $F''(s).$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5t}={F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{30{s}^{2}+250}{({s}^{2}-25)^{3}},\\,s>5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221150 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb99e6f5-005f-543f-b6c9-a0dc1c304d99" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "d0a7789e-bed4-5bb5-af46-cdebec0fe8cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-6643" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathcal L\\left\\{t^n\\right\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = (t - 5)^2$ for $s > 0.$" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\alpha f(t) + \\beta g(t)\\} = \\alpha\\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\} + \\beta\\cdot \\mathcal L \\{g(t)\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants.\n\nFirst, we expand:\n\n$$\nf(t) = (t - 5)^2= t^2 - 10t + 25\n$$\n\nNow, taking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{t}^{2}-10t+25} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}+L{-10t}+L{25} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}-10L{t}+25L{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\mathcal L\\left\\{t^n\\right\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}.$ Therefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{t}^{2}}-10L{t}+25L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-10\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+25\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-\\frac{10}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{25}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-10s+25{s}^{2}}{{s}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{25{s}^{2}-10s+2}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb99e6f5-005f-543f-b6c9-a0dc1c304d99" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bc91834-b8b9-5902-93d4-7bd7872491ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{10}{s^2}+\\frac{50}{s^3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bc91834-b8b9-5902-93d4-7bd7872491ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{10}{s^2}+\\frac{50}{s^3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374822} :answer-field/id #uuid "92e21b0b-3b4f-5210-9f9e-3893a248bd4e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8687e09-4a9b-574b-9a7c-ec2576578674" :question/math-academy-id "q-330949" :question/problem "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=(1-5t)^{2}$ for $s>0.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, we expand:\n\n$$\nf(t)=(1-5t)^{2}=1-10t+25{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{1-10t+25{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =L{1}+L{-10t}+L{25{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =L{1}-10L{t}+25L{{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.$ Therefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{1}-10L{t}+25L{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}-10\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+25\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{10}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{50}{{s}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-10s+50}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb99e6f5-005f-543f-b6c9-a0dc1c304d99" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93e0a201-7d83-5228-b814-8bb22803d22a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{8}{s^2}+\\frac{32}{s^3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374825} {:answer/id #uuid "522d44ff-b570-5091-8188-a4686dbe72fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{8}{s^2}+\\frac{16}{s^3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93e0a201-7d83-5228-b814-8bb22803d22a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{8}{s^2}+\\frac{32}{s^3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374825} :answer-field/id #uuid "b92aefc4-bbdd-5c96-b311-51606bfbd9eb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5f2c666-771b-56c8-b6f5-ee6fa5a95a99" :question/math-academy-id "q-330950" :question/problem "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=(4t+1)^{2}$ for $s>0.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, we expand:\n\n$$\nf(t)=(4t+1)^{2}=16{t}^{2}+8t+1\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{16{t}^{2}+8t+1} \\\\  & =L{16{t}^{2}}+L{8t}+L{1} \\\\  & =16L{{t}^{2}}+8L{t}+L{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.$ Therefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =16L{{t}^{2}}+8L{t}+L{1} \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{8}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+8s+32}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb99e6f5-005f-543f-b6c9-a0dc1c304d99" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc38d939-119d-5556-8079-631eaae3ef9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{s^4}-\\frac{4}{s^2}+\\frac{5}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc38d939-119d-5556-8079-631eaae3ef9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{s^4}-\\frac{4}{s^2}+\\frac{5}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374829} :answer-field/id #uuid "89f491f4-a4c0-5cdd-97f1-f5e3f209cee7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff6f2cd5-eded-50b5-8611-bb75eb5d2094" :question/math-academy-id "q-331109" :question/problem "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={t}^{3}-4t+5$ for $s>0.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}-4t+5} & =L{{t}^{3}}+L{-4t}+L{5} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{3}}-4L{t}+5L{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.$ Therefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{t}^{3}} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{t}^{3}}-4L{t}+5L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}-\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{s}^{3}-4{s}^{2}+6}{{s}^{4}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb99e6f5-005f-543f-b6c9-a0dc1c304d99" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "329ef788-52c8-54c4-9a91-8881e1070f2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{s^2}+\\frac{s}{s^2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "329ef788-52c8-54c4-9a91-8881e1070f2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{s^2}+\\frac{s}{s^2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374832} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ce44644-a051-548d-a09e-261b5916f4c5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9e04e758-7856-51ec-b556-dbdba83f9cbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-331645" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0 \\\\ L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=-3t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ for $s>0.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{-3t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =L{-3t}+L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-3L{t}+L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =-3L{t}+L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{s\\cdot {s}^{2}-3({s}^{2}+1)}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{3}-3{s}^{2}-3}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+1)},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32e63c33-8820-542f-afab-f27a1f6a847a" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Smoothness Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbe23aab-3ac9-5e56-8391-603e93861281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the Laplace transform" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374835} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce0b010-6ebf-5be3-bdf6-519530244799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374836} {:answer/id #uuid "4bcdd6e2-9a1d-5f68-aac3-73e22cac840e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integration by parts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374837} {:answer/id #uuid "27b91b1a-613a-5a8c-8f70-1f3b7c967f57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the derivative with respect to $s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27b91b1a-613a-5a8c-8f70-1f3b7c967f57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the derivative with respect to $s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374838} :answer-field/id #uuid "013fe86e-8bb9-5d77-9d83-266e528dac0c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374834} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c675054-8051-5b95-99b8-6dde405f730b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign again" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374840} {:answer/id #uuid "206824af-82cd-59b9-a5bc-5d3603bb41e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integration by parts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374841} {:answer/id #uuid "09eba3af-ebfc-574c-a9d6-5a28de68b5ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09eba3af-ebfc-574c-a9d6-5a28de68b5ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374842} :answer-field/id #uuid "34dd63af-fa3b-56b8-b0a6-65904b80a619" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374839} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97e34c1d-3f42-59fc-b707-a8331fd57c46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the chain rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374844} {:answer/id #uuid "81cadb11-d71b-50d5-b6d8-5f2cf254e6d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the Laplace transform" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374845} {:answer/id #uuid "4bac70b0-af50-5eca-a2fe-d1562ba175c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the product rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81cadb11-d71b-50d5-b6d8-5f2cf254e6d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the Laplace transform" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374845} :answer-field/id #uuid "15f6d32d-580b-5795-9e1d-284ef0e9f4f6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70e96342-9f6f-5f4d-885c-91889dc5a387" :question/math-academy-id "q-334829" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=-\\,L{t\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))=-t{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }t{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=-L{t\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ and\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n=1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L2}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we know that\n\n$$\nF(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nBy $\\text{the definition of the derivative with respect to }s$, we get\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{d}{ds}F(s)=\\frac{d}{ds}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the statement in line $\\text{L2}.$\n\n- Next we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nLine $\\text{L4}$ tells us that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))=-t{e}^{-st}f(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression into the integrand in line $\\text{L3}$ gives\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-t{e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }t{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{substitution}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L6}.$ By $\\text{the definition of the Laplace transform},$\n\n$$\nL{t\\cdot f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}(tf(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the integral in line $\\text{L5}$, we see that\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }t{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}(tf(t))\\,dt=-\\,L{t\\cdot f(t)},\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the expression in line $\\text{L6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32e63c33-8820-542f-afab-f27a1f6a847a" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Smoothness Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5edcfd94-d0da-5db3-911e-4d4746c4aa9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the Laplace transform is linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374849} {:answer/id #uuid "125feffa-1de6-5e3c-816d-e847bbad5de4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${e}^{-st}$ is infinitely differentiable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374850} {:answer/id #uuid "e138716a-243c-574e-a4f7-f515b631d7c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ is bounded on $[0,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374851} {:answer/id #uuid "7e30ed24-8be2-55b1-ab44-622421754eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374852} {:answer/id #uuid "3b5ccc21-cb9a-5f32-8e9c-4938ca1b517c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ is of exponential order and is piecewise continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b5ccc21-cb9a-5f32-8e9c-4938ca1b517c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ is of exponential order and is piecewise continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374853} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb400239-b6f2-50c6-8b8d-5aea30d6e344" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374848} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f2af38c-7762-5868-8d56-0cb6920c1445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the Laplace transform" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374855} {:answer/id #uuid "f51f8e39-b29e-5eee-9ca2-dbd22e5e7b7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374856} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4f7656-477a-52de-ad29-714c6fa97f0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integration by parts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374857} {:answer/id #uuid "99b9a4f6-61cf-5c11-ab2e-2a42d12144c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the derivative with respect to $s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99b9a4f6-61cf-5c11-ab2e-2a42d12144c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the derivative with respect to $s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374858} :answer-field/id #uuid "37596cff-2fad-5c57-9479-ed09e2f6c0d3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374854} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a0da89a-fd37-54b5-b307-f8986fc080cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the continuity of partial derivatives" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374860} {:answer/id #uuid "1b493123-16a3-59e2-8202-ee0a5ac28dd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374861} {:answer/id #uuid "f74ec841-ef67-5418-b31b-72dfe3a17162" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integration by parts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b493123-16a3-59e2-8202-ee0a5ac28dd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374861} :answer-field/id #uuid "85872ffd-dd20-5e36-a01b-a119be73fa37" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc74e83d-6a57-5b4d-aabe-8493114a6c91" :question/math-academy-id "q-334832" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$, has exponential order $a,$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=-\\,L{t\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))=-t{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }t{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=-L{t\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ The integral in line $\\text{L1}$ converges since\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n=1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1}.$ Since $f\\text{ is of exponential order and is piecewise continuous}$, \nthere exist constants $K,T>0$ and $a$ such that\n\n$$\n|f(t)|\\le K{e}^{at},\\,t>T.\n$$\n\nWhen computing the Laplace transform, we multiply $f(t)$ by ${e}^{-st}.$ If $s>a$, then for all sufficiently large $t$,\n\n$$\n|{e}^{-st}f(t)|\\le K{e}^{-(s-a)t}.\n$$\n\nBecause $s-a>0$, the function ${e}^{-(s-a)t}$ is integrable on $[T,\\infty )$, and\n$f$ is bounded on $[0,T].$\nTherefore, the entire integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\nconverges. This justifies the expression in line $\\text{L1}.$\n\n- We now consider line $\\text{L2}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we know that\n\n$$\nF(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nBy $\\text{the definition of the derivative with respect to }s$, we get\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{d}{ds}F(s)=\\frac{d}{ds}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the statement in line $\\text{L2}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{d}{ds}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is of exponential order, the integral converges, and we can\ndifferentiate under the integral sign:\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{differentiating under the integral sign}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32e63c33-8820-542f-afab-f27a1f6a847a" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Smoothness Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58157f89-cd52-5c12-93c3-0fb167c50d26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the Laplace transform is linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374865} {:answer/id #uuid "7d84c0fe-4512-5d7e-8082-3930c6030a7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${e}^{-st}$ is infinitely differentiable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374866} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d46375-8ebf-5496-a682-985252f0075b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374867} {:answer/id #uuid "72876e78-6c6a-5532-bc71-d04b85714610" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ is of exponential order and is piecewise continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374868} {:answer/id #uuid "99d4184c-b2d1-55d1-b7f9-b4fac13d6bbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ is of exponential order" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72876e78-6c6a-5532-bc71-d04b85714610" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ is of exponential order and is piecewise continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374868} :answer-field/id #uuid "267687e6-dd6d-5ce6-9d6d-c6d4ced55018" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374864} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9937181-8be9-5d19-9037-2ab960415cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the continuity of partial derivatives" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374871} {:answer/id #uuid "899a7a7d-7d6b-5f76-90d8-2d675006cac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374872} {:answer/id #uuid "b85c23c0-586b-5214-878e-e77d668a881d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrating by parts with respect to $s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "899a7a7d-7d6b-5f76-90d8-2d675006cac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374872} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf16211f-0078-5d65-8ca2-b4d9cc6bfada" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374870} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a57703-bacf-5258-b81f-eb2d3f10efd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign again" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374875} {:answer/id #uuid "5f188531-4105-5e0c-a601-d4c0defe8f7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integration by parts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374876} {:answer/id #uuid "aacd15ff-93c3-59a4-bfdb-368617596ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aacd15ff-93c3-59a4-bfdb-368617596ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374877} :answer-field/id #uuid "53bf41fd-214f-529b-878b-e55c5b604404" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd9a9bd1-b77a-509e-89d0-e81ab3f3fe64" :question/math-academy-id "q-334876" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$, has exponential order $a,$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ The integral in line $\\text{L1}$ converges since\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ and\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n=2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1}.$ Since $f\\text{ is of exponential order and is piecewise continuous}$, \nthere exist constants $K,T>0$ and $a$ such that\n\n$$\n|f(t)|\\le K{e}^{at},\\,t>T.\n$$\n\nWhen computing the Laplace transform, we multiply $f(t)$ by ${e}^{-st}.$ If $s>a$, then for all sufficiently large $t$,\n\n$$\n|{e}^{-st}f(t)|\\le K{e}^{-(s-a)t}.\n$$\n\nBecause $s-a>0$, the function ${e}^{-(s-a)t}$ is integrable on $[T,\\infty )$, and\n$f$ is bounded on $[0,T].$\nTherefore, the entire integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\nconverges. This justifies the expression in line $\\text{L1}.$\n\n- Next we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is of exponential order, the integral converges, and we can\ndifferentiate under the integral sign:\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{differentiating under the integral sign}.$\n\n- We now consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nLine $\\text{L4}$ tells us that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression into the integrand in line $\\text{L3}$ gives\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{substitution}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32e63c33-8820-542f-afab-f27a1f6a847a" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Smoothness Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ef34a2b-2dcb-57c0-ba9b-85299b371002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the Laplace transform" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374880} {:answer/id #uuid "52ec0738-2c06-51be-98fc-7120a70dcb34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374881} {:answer/id #uuid "d2ef4684-5067-5a3d-abf5-ce7cb8e0907e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the second derivative with respect to $s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374882} {:answer/id #uuid "c96bd4a9-587f-5b60-bc2d-60bf40863808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integration by parts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2ef4684-5067-5a3d-abf5-ce7cb8e0907e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the second derivative with respect to $s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374882} :answer-field/id #uuid "602e2f73-7e0c-5a20-bbf2-f77bb64967c1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374879} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a174f9b8-e850-51c3-bb5a-ca8ff1a9d846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the continuity of partial derivatives" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374885} {:answer/id #uuid "4399b361-692c-5165-944b-3e1ce8075660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374886} {:answer/id #uuid "0c08cee8-fda2-576e-9cde-45e0b0e7d50e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrating by parts with respect to $s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4399b361-692c-5165-944b-3e1ce8075660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374886} :answer-field/id #uuid "c863680d-7819-514a-aa98-5fbdd6e7069d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374884} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c38f443f-ab7c-566e-b939-a42768b4de07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign again" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374889} {:answer/id #uuid "1be917e9-ca89-57af-bdf7-9bbeb970ebdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integration by parts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374890} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6c0206-2cfe-5c37-8d1b-d417d0973eec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c6c0206-2cfe-5c37-8d1b-d417d0973eec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374891} :answer-field/id #uuid "32b03502-27aa-501a-9eb3-9553301ed7a7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b669529-7292-59be-8712-06b8b95e08ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-334879" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then $F(s)$ is twice differentiable for all $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ exists for $s>a.$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}$  \n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ and\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n=2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L2}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we know that\n\n$$\nF(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nBy $\\text{the definition of the second derivative with respect to }s$, we get\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}F(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the statement in line $\\text{L2}.$\n\n- Next we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is of exponential order, the integral converges, and we can\ndifferentiate under the integral sign:\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{differentiating under the integral sign}.$\n\n- We now consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nLine $\\text{L4}$ tells us that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression into the integrand in line $\\text{L3}$ gives\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{substitution}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32e63c33-8820-542f-afab-f27a1f6a847a" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Smoothness Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98eb4783-50b8-5a7e-9e56-e0b89cd20c1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374894} {:answer/id #uuid "290b6ecb-59bb-529d-9c94-8243bdc81ba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374895} {:answer/id #uuid "2c19d9b1-b927-54b7-ad71-380d9007826a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374896} {:answer/id #uuid "baaafd83-6435-5481-ba45-1b6dc0622db6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374897} {:answer/id #uuid "220f36c9-9667-56dd-b141-500bfeeecd25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98eb4783-50b8-5a7e-9e56-e0b89cd20c1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374894} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff884d80-5eb4-5fb5-b64e-f8bfc9d3c4a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2371d6e5-f082-541f-8d89-2e4919579628" :question/math-academy-id "q-334881" :question/problem "Let $f(t)$ be a piecewise continuous function on $[0,\\infty )$ with exponential order $a,$ and consider its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)},\\,s>a.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $f(t)$ is of exponential order $a,$ then the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ converges for every $s>a.$\n- If $f(t)$ is of exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform $F(s)$ exists for all $s>a.$\n- The Laplace transform of any piecewise continuous function of exponential order is infinitely differentiable in its region of convergence." :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence.\n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and every $s>a,$\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f$ is of exponential order and is piecewise continuous, there exist constants $K,T>0$ and $a$ such that\n\n$$\n|f(t)|\\le K{e}^{at},\\,t>T.\n$$\n\nWhen computing the Laplace transform, we multiply $f(t)$ by ${e}^{-st}.$ If $s>a$, then for all sufficiently large $t$,\n\n$$\n|{e}^{-st}f(t)|\\le K{e}^{-(s-a)t}.\n$$\n\nBecause $s-a>0$, the function ${e}^{-(s-a)t}$ is integrable on $[T,\\infty )$, and\n$f$ is bounded on $[0,T].$\nTherefore, the entire integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\nconverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. The standard existence theorem for the Laplace transform says that if $f$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and of exponential order $a,$ then the improper integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\nconverges for every $s>a.$ This is exactly what it means for $F(s)$ to exist for all $s>a.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The smoothness theorem guarantees\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}\n$$\n\nfor every $n\\ge 0$ and each $s>a,$ which shows that $F(s)$ is infinitely differentiable, even if $f(t)$ itself is not.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7114bb44-4dd5-5b2b-86dc-f81761c0e07a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms Using the Smoothness Property (1st Derivative)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22600925-a217-5728-bf66-8fde5103b5da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{F}^{′}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374901} {:answer/id #uuid "ca93d22d-aa1c-5804-bfc7-65abca9c931e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s{F}^{′}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374902} {:answer/id #uuid "28d6b6a1-9b3d-5cff-ad68-16129c9d4851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-F(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374903} {:answer/id #uuid "f4354666-1a9c-5acf-bf0c-9130ade9608a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-s{F}^{′}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374904} {:answer/id #uuid "0e90eb37-2051-5c3e-b2e1-10428355b50c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{F}^{′}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22600925-a217-5728-bf66-8fde5103b5da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{F}^{′}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374901} :answer-field/id #uuid "41dc947d-00e6-5682-96b6-542c305ae0c5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374900} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d332a3dc-cbee-5acc-ad82-004f9f8b8c93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374907} {:answer/id #uuid "dd09bbaf-5c9c-559e-adb2-1b30e140e162" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374908} {:answer/id #uuid "78916b17-bc87-5c94-a0d5-6b9f3e0e6bff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374909} {:answer/id #uuid "3b0d005a-0579-5211-a2eb-e35f5f6a593b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b0d005a-0579-5211-a2eb-e35f5f6a593b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374910} :answer-field/id #uuid "70542c54-9b92-5564-9c9b-3b6811940333" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374906} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e17b70de-5b68-50a1-9ff9-07ffaf882e79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16-{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374912} {:answer/id #uuid "94fb5b76-48ac-51ec-b755-c3422c5155bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{s}^{2}-16}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374913} {:answer/id #uuid "1dae4442-3bce-5df3-ba5f-ca11c21f2b44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2s}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374914} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ce270e-2f64-588c-a3bb-1ce80d1037b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2s}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374915} {:answer/id #uuid "ed20cd2d-26ec-506c-90a4-766dbec1ce84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16s}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e17b70de-5b68-50a1-9ff9-07ffaf882e79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16-{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374912} :answer-field/id #uuid "06e94970-20b3-51b7-acfb-62b0e49ee028" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374911} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb660cee-7ab3-5bbd-a416-9d790377921e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{s}^{2}-4}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374918} {:answer/id #uuid "f8a80120-f73b-5932-be23-3d764209e56c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16-{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374919} {:answer/id #uuid "0b19bd0d-0595-5c61-addd-157411387959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{s}^{2}-16}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374920} {:answer/id #uuid "4b6328ee-e5f4-5756-900c-8788382ae196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4-{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b19bd0d-0595-5c61-addd-157411387959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{s}^{2}-16}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374920} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd14fa5f-95fc-59b1-89d0-2ffdd28bedaa" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ed09e66-38d6-559c-aeca-d2a926b0d657" :question/math-academy-id "q-340899" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to calculate the Laplace transform \n\n$$\nL{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}.\n$$\n\n \nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below. You may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nLet $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t$ and $L{f(t)}=F(s).$ By the smoothness property of Laplace transforms,\n\n$$\nL{tf(t)}={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={{field-2}},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}={{field-4}},\\,s>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{n}f(t)}=(-1)^{n}{F}^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{tf(t)}=-{F}^{′}(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t.$ Using the given result with $\\omega =4,$ the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(s({s}^{2}+16)^{-1}) \\\\  & =({s}^{2}+16)^{-1}+s(-({s}^{2}+16)^{-2}\\cdot 2s) \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+16}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}-\\frac{2{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{16-{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}=-{F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}-16}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7114bb44-4dd5-5b2b-86dc-f81761c0e07a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms Using the Smoothness Property (1st Derivative)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bc65a86-25c6-55a9-a4d5-dc79261c423b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{F}^{′}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374924} {:answer/id #uuid "963dc65c-4e77-5289-9c3a-81da91414b25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s{F}^{′}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374925} {:answer/id #uuid "e7bde637-b6ee-5100-a3d2-87a6f3385b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-F(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374926} {:answer/id #uuid "b9ed2c0f-5768-5403-be8e-bdf0f8ceedc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-s{F}^{′}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374927} {:answer/id #uuid "1b8bb59d-7bd0-51ee-a445-553e9572564b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{F}^{′}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bc65a86-25c6-55a9-a4d5-dc79261c423b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{F}^{′}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374924} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c927b00-fe73-57d7-bf43-a389497c6fd0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374923} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bba67c9b-d22e-5c58-b88c-35c7463fba