derate} :question/id #uuid "b2eeb45e-7256-5380-b44d-edda880700e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-341222" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to calculate the Laplace transform \n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(-6t)}.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below. You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>\\omega .\n$$\n\nLet $f(t)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(-6t)$ and $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$ By the smoothness property of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}f(t)}={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(-6t)=-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6t,$ the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={{field-2}},\\,s>6.\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(-6t)}={{field-4}},\\,s>6.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{n}f(t)}=(-1)^{n}{F}^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=2,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}f(t)}={F}^{″}(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nf(t)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(-6t)=-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6t.\n$$\n\nUsing the given result with $\\omega =6,$ the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6t$ is $\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-36},$ so\n\n$$\nF(s)=-\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-36},\\,s>6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-36}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(6({s}^{2}-36)^{-1}) \\\\  & =-(-6({s}^{2}-36)^{-2}\\cdot 2s) \\\\  & =\\frac{12s}{({s}^{2}-36)^{2}} \\\\ {F}^{″}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{12s}{({s}^{2}-36)^{2}}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}-36)^{2}}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{(s)^{′}\\cdot ({s}^{2}-36)^{2}-s\\cdot {(({s}^{2}-36)^{2})}^{′}}{({s}^{2}-36)^{4}}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{({s}^{2}-36)^{2}-s\\cdot 2({s}^{2}-36)\\cdot 2s}{({s}^{2}-36)^{4}}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{({s}^{2}-36)^{2}-4{s}^{2}({s}^{2}-36)}{({s}^{2}-36)^{4}}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{{s}^{2}-36-4{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}-36)^{3}}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{-3{s}^{2}-36}{({s}^{2}-36)^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{36{s}^{2}+432}{({s}^{2}-36)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the quotient rule to find ${F}^{″}(s).$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(-6t)}={F}^{″}(s)=-\\frac{36{s}^{2}+432}{({s}^{2}-36)^{3}},\\,s>6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374988 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb99e6f5-005f-543f-b6c9-a0dc1c304d99" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06d4e344-61f6-5cfc-acce-c59f17383336" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{s({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374990} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3b5e08-8667-5e14-ac78-9a1114c055de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{s}^{2}+15}{s(s+9)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374991} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb88adb-e9e4-595d-ad65-1a1842e551d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{s}^{2}+27}{s({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374992} {:answer/id #uuid "7c04392a-43ad-5680-9aa3-9117d154fe60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{s}^{2}+1}{s(s+9)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374993} {:answer/id #uuid "ee488264-0e80-5836-b283-178dcd0a70b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4s+27}{s({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbb88adb-e9e4-595d-ad65-1a1842e551d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{s}^{2}+27}{s({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374992} :answer-field/id #uuid "052ae53b-68b4-52a7-a343-f8c248c3840e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ad0fdd2c-5421-5879-ba93-fabc463ecc2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-89595" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0 \\\\ L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=3-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t$ for $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{3-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t} \\\\  & =L{3}-L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t} \\\\  & =3L{1}-L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =3L{1}-L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({s}^{2}+9)-{s}^{2}}{s({s}^{2}+9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{s}^{2}+27-{s}^{2}}{s({s}^{2}+9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{s}^{2}+27}{s({s}^{2}+9)},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
